#Reserved Words 
# these words have special meaning to the shell. They are used to begin and end the shell's compound commands.

#The following are reserved when unquoted and the first word of a command
if then elif else fi time for in until while do done case esac coproc select function { } [[ ]] !
#in is a reserved word if it is the third word of a case or select command. in and do are recognized as reserved
#words if they are the third word in a for command.

#simple commands
#simple commands are commands that are executed most often. It is just a sequence of words separated by blanks,
#terminated by one of the shell's control operations. The first word generally specifies a command to be executed,
#the remaining words are the arguments for that command.
#the return status for simple commands is the exit status provided by POSIX 1003.1 waitpid function, or 128+n if
# the command was terminated by signal n.

#Pipelines
#a pipeline is one or more commands separated by the control operators '|' or '|&'
#The format
[time [-p]] [!] command1 [ | or |& command2] ...
#the output of each command in the pipe is connected via a pipe to the input of the next command.
#The connection is performed by any redirection specified by command1
#'|&' is the pipeline operator; it connects  command1's standard error and standard output tto command2's standard
# input through the pipe (shorthand for 2>&1 |). The standard error redirection happens after redirections specified
# by command1 (is consistent with shorthand).
#Using the preserved word time before the pipeline prints timing statistics for the pipeline ince it finishes.
#The stats consist of elapsed (wall-clock) time and user and system time used by the command's execution.
#-p changes the output format to the POSIX specification. In POSIX mode, the shell doesnt treat time as a reserved
# word if the next token begins with -. The TIMEFORMAT variable is a format string that specifies how the 
#timing information should be displayed. If time is used, we are also able to time shell builtins, shell functions,
#and pipelines. External time commands can't easily time those.
#In POSIX mode, the time command can be used by itself and prints total user time consumed by the sell and its 
#children. The TIMEFORMAT variable specifies the the format of the time information
#For non-asynchronous execution, the shell waits for all commands in the pipeline to complete.
#Individual commands in multi-command pipelines are executed in their own subshells, which are separate processes.
#enabling the lastpipe option using the shopt builtin without job control active, then the last element of a 
#pipeline may run by the shell process.
#The exit status for a pipeline is the exit status for of its last command in the pipeline, except when the pipefail 
#option is enabled. ppipefail makes the pipelines exit status equal to the rightmost command to exit with non-zero 
#status, or zero if all commands exit successfully. If the reserved word '!' precedes the pipeline, then the exit
# status is the logical negation. For non-asynchronous execution, the shell waits for all commands to finish before
#returning a value. asynchronous pipelines have a retiurn status of 0.

#Lists of commands
# a list is any number of pipelines eeparated by one or more of the following operators: ‘;’, ‘&’, ‘&&’, 
#or ‘||’, and optionally terminated by one of ‘;’, ‘&’, or a newline.  
#&& and || have equal precedence, then ; and &.
#one or more newlines may appear in a list to delimit commands, like semicolon.
#terminating a command with the control operator & causes the shell to execute the command asynchronously in a
#subshell. The shell doesnt wait for the command to finish and the return status is 0. Without job control active
#the standard input for asynchronous commands, when explicit redirection is absent, is redirected from /dev/null
#commands separated by ; are executed sequentially; the shell waits for each command to terminate in turn. The 
#return status is the exit status of the last command executed.
#AND and OR lists are sequences of one or more pipelines separated by the control operators '&&' and '||', 
#respectively. AND and OR lists are executed with left associativity. 
#AND list form
command1 && command2
//command2 executed only if command1 returns exit status zero
#OR list form
command1 || command2
//command2 executed only if command 1 returns a non-zero exit status

#AND and OR lists exit status is the exit status of the last command in the list

#Compound Commands
#compound commands are the shell programming language constructs. Each begins with a reserved word or control
# operator and is terminated by a corresponding reserved word or operator. Any redirections associated with 
#a compound command applies to all the commands within that compound command unless explicit override used.
#a list of commands in a complex command's description may be separated from the rest of the command by one or
#more newlines, and can be followed by a newline in place of a semicolon.

#Bash provides looping mechanisms, conditional commands, and mechanics to group commands and execute them as a \
#unit.

#looping constructs
#Whenever a ; appears in looping constructs you can replace it with a one or more newlines
#until command
until test-commands; do consequent-commands; done
#executes consequent-commands as long as the test-commands have an exit status not zero. The return status is 
#the exit status of the last command executed in consequent-commands, or zero if none was executed.
#while command
while test-commands; do consequent-commands; done
#executes consequent commands as long as test-commands has an exit status of zero.
#The return status is the exit status of the last command executed in consequent-commands, or zero if none was
#executed. 
#for command
for name [ [in words ...] ; ] do commands; done
#expands words and executes each command once for each word in the resultant list; name is bound to the 
#current word from the list. If in words not present, then for command executes commands once for each
#positional parameter that is set, as if in $@ had been specified. 
#The return status is the exit status of the last command that executes. If there are no items in the 
#expansion of words, no commands are executed, and the return status is zero
#the alternate form of for which resembles C's for is
for ((exp1 ; exp2 ; exp3 )) [;] do commands ; done
#First evaluates the arithmetic expression exp1, then repeatedly evaluates the arithmetic exp2 until it evaluates
# to zero. Each time exp2 evaluates to a non-zero value, execute commands and evaluate the arithmetic expression
#exp3. Ommitting any expression treats that position as evaluating to 1. The return value is the exit status of
# the last command in commands that is executed, or non-zero if any of expressions is invalid.

#conditional constructs
#if command
if test-commands; then 
   consequent-commands;
[elif more-test-commands; then
   more-consequents;]
[else alternate-consequents;]
fi

#test-commands list is executed, and if its return status is zero, the consequent-commands list is executed. 
#if test-commands returns non-zero status, each elif list is executed in turn, and if its exit status is zero
#, the corresponding more-consequents is executed and the command completes. If else alternate-consequents is
#present, and the final command in the final if or elif clause has a non-zero exit status, then alternate-consequents is executed. The return status is the exit status of the last executed command, or zero if no 
#condition tested true.

#case command
case word in
   [ [ (] pattern [| pattern]...) command-list ;;]...
esac
#case selectively executes the command-list correponding to the first pattern that matches word,
#fro first pattern to the last. If the nocasematch shell option is enabled (shopt builtin used for this),
# then matching is performed without
#regard to the case of alphabetic characters. The '|' is used to separate multiple patterns in a pattern list,
# and the ')' operator terminates the pattern list. The oattern list and associated command-list is termed
#a clause
#Clauses must rerminate with either of ';;', ';&' , or ';;&'. The first matching pattern determines the 
#command-list that is executed. It is common to use * as the final pattern to define default case becuase the 
#pattern always matches.
#example 
echo -n "Enter the name of an animal:"
read ANIMAL
echo -n "The $ANIMAL has "
case $ANIMAL in
   horse | dog | cat) echo -n "four";;
   man | kangaroo ) echo -n "two";;
   *) echo -n "an unknown number of";;
esac
echo " legs."

#If the ;; operator is used, then the case command finishes after first match. The ;& causes execution 
#continuation with the command-list of the next clause, if any. ;;&makes the shell test the patterns in the next
# clause and execute any associated command-list if pattern matched.
#no match returns exit status of zero, else exit status is the exit status of the last command-list executed.
#select command
#allows easy selection of menus
#almost same syntax as for command
select name [in words ...]; do commands; done
#expands the list of words, generating a list of items, and prints the set of expanded words to the standard 
#error stream, each preceded by a number. If in words is ommitted, then rint the positional parameters as if
#in $@ used. Select displays the PS3 prompt and reads a line from standard input. If the line contains a number
#correponding to one of the displayed words, then select sets the value of name to that word. If the line
#is empty, select displays the words and prompt again. If EOF is reached, then select completes and returns
# 1. Anything else causes name to be set to null. The line read is saved in the variable REPLY.
#commands are executed after each selection until a break command is executed, at which point select completes
#example of user picking a filename from the current directory and displaying anme and index of file:
select fname in *;
do
   echo you picked $fname \($REPLY\)
   break;
done

#((expression))
#the expression exapnds the same as if it was in double quotes, but unescaped double quote characters
#in expression are not treated specially and are removed. This can potentially lead to empty strings, those cases
#are treated as evaluations to 0. If the value of the expression is not zero, then the return status is 0; 
#otherwise the return status is 1.

#[[ expression ]]
#evaluates the conditional expression and returns a status of zero (true) or non-zero (false).
#The words between [[ and ]] do not undergo word splitting and filename expansion. The shell performs tilde
#expansion, parameter and variable expansion, arithmetic expansion, command substitution, process substitution
#and quote removal on those words. Conditional operators such as '-f' must be unquoted to be recognized as
#primaries.
#when used with the [[, the '<' and '>' operators sort lexicographically using the current locale.
#when the '==' and '1==' are used, the string to the right is considered a pattern and matched, as if the 
#extglob shell option were enabled. The '=' operator is identical to '=='. If the nocasematch shell option
# (shopt builtin used) is enabled, then pattern matching ignores case of alphabetic characters.
#The return value 0 is for string matches == or no match for != the pattern, and 1 otherwise.
#Quoting any part of the pattern using any of the shell's quoting mechanisms causes the quoted portion
#to be matched literally. So every character in the quoted portion matches itself, no special pattern matching
#meaning.
#the =~ binary carries the same precedence as == and !=. Its usage matches right-hand side strings according
#to POSIX extended regular expression pattern (uses POSIX regcomp and regexec).  The return value is 0 if 
#the string matches the pattern, and 1 if it does not. If the regular expression syntax is not correct, then 
#the return value is 2. Again the nocasematch shell option can be enabled.
#quoting any part of the pattern makes the quoted portion match literally instead of as a regular expression.
#If stored in a shell variable, quoting the variable expansion forces the entire pattern to be matched literally.
#The match succeeds if the pattern matches any part of the string. If you want to force the pattern to
#match the entire string, anchor the pattern using the ^ and $ regular expression operators.
#Example:the below code matches a line (stored in shell variable line) if there is a sequence of characters 
#anywhere in the value consisting of any number, including zero, of characters in the space character class
#immediately followed by zero or one instance of 'a', then a 'b'.
[[ $line =~ [[:space:]]*(a)?b ]]
#‘aab’, ‘ aaaaaab’, ‘xaby’, and ‘ ab’ will all match, as will a line containing a ‘b’ anywhere in its value. 
#matching characters that are special to the regular expression grammar (^$|[]()\,*+?'), has to be quoted to remove
# the special meaning. Example the dot in xxx.txt matches any character, but in "xxx.txt" only matches the literal 
#dot. 
#Also, if you want to include a character in your pattern that has a special meaning to the regular expression
#grammar, you must make sure it is not quoted. To anchor a pattern at the beginning or end of the string, you can
#use the '^' or '$' using any form of shell quoting.
#example matching string at the start of a line
[[ $line =~ ^"initial string" ]]
#but this will not work:
[[ $line =~ "^initial string" ]]
#specifying regular expression without using quotes can be difficult, and tracking quoting used by regular 
#expressions while paying attention to the shell quoting and the shell's quote removal  is equal as challenging. 
#Storing regular expressions in variables is a good work around the complextiy of managing quoting characters
# special to the shell.
#example equivalent to previous examples
pattern=' [[:space:]]*(a)?b'
[[ $line =~ $pattern ]]
#backslashes are used by regular expressions and the shell, care must be taken to account for remaining backslash 
#characters that were not quoted after the shell expansion, as they can remove the meaning of special pattern
#characters. The shell does its best to ensure that quoted patterns have backslashes in those quoted areas 
#remain literal.
#example - the seet of commands are not equal
pattern='\.'
[[ . =~ $pattern ]]
[[ . =~ \. ]]

[[ . =~ "$pattern" ]]
[[ . =~ '\.' ]] 
#the first two matches succeed, but the second two will not, because these backslashes will be treated as literals
#that are part of the actual patter (not treated as special characters). The first \. uses the backslash to escape 
#the special character dot, leaving only the literal dot (the backslash is removed), while the second \. treats the
#string as a literal consisting of a \ and a dot. 
#Bracket expressions in regular expressions can be sources of errors, since characters that are normally special
#in regular expressions lose their special meanings between brackets. However, bracket expression can also be used
#to match special pattern characters without quoting them.
[[ . =~ [.] ]]
#The shell performs any word expansions before passing the pattern to the regular expression functions, so you can
#assume that the shell's quoting takes precedence. The regular expression  parser will interpret any unquoted
#backslashes remaining in the pattern after shell  expansion acccording to its own rules. 
#The array var BASH_REMATCH records which parts of the string matched the pattern. The element of BASH_REMATCH
#with index 0 contains the portion of the string matching the entire regular expression. Substrings matched by
#by parenthesized subexpressions within the regular expression are saved in the remaining BASH_REMATCH indices. 
#The element of BASH_REMATCH with index n is the portion of the string matching the nth parenthesized subexpression.
#bash sets BASH_REMATCH in the global scope, dont declare it as a local variable.
