#grouping commands
#two ways to group commands to be executed as a unit. Grouped commands have redirection applied to the entire 
#command list. 

( list )
#placing commands in parenthesis forces the shell to create a subshell, where each of the parenthesized commands is
#executed in the environment of the subshell.
{list ;}
#placing commands in curly braces causes them to be executed in the current shell environment. No subshell created.
#The semicolon at the end is mandatory.

#braces are treated as reserved words by the shell, so need to be separated from the list by blanks or other
#metacharacters. Parenthesis are operators, and are recognized as separate tokens by the shell even if not 
#separated from the list by whitespace.
#The exit status of both constructs is the same as the one for the list.

#coprocesses
#A coprocess is a shell command preceded by the coprocess reserved word. Coprocesses are executed asynchronously in 
#a subshell, as if the command was terminated by an & control operator, with a two-way pipe established between the
# executing shell and the coprocess.

#syntax 
coproc [NAME] command {redirection]
#here a coprocess called NAME is made; command can be simple or a compound command. NAME is a shell variable; if
#not supplied the default is COPROC.
#recommended form
coproc NAME { command; } 
#this form is simpler than the other compound commands, and becase simple commands always result in the coprocess
#being named COPROC.
coproc NAME compound-command
coproc compound-command
coproc simple-command
#compound commands can optionally ommit NAME. The word following coproc determines if that word is a variable name,
#which happens when  it is not a reserved word that introduces a compound command. For simple commands, NAME is not
#allowed; this avoids confusion between NAME and the first word of the simple command.
#when the coprocess executed, the shell creates an array variable named NMAE in the context of the current shell.
#The standard output of command is connected via a pipe to a file descriptor in the executing shell; the files
#descriptor is assigned to NAME[0]. Similarly, standard input of a command is connected via a pipe to file
#descriptor in the executing shell assigned to NAME[1]. The pipe is specified before any redirections specified by 
#the command. The file descriptors can be utilized as arguments to shell commands and redirections using standard
# word expansions. Other than those created to execute command and process substitution, the file descriptors are
#not available in subshells.
#The process ID of the spawned subshell is available as the value of the variable NAME_PID. The wait builtin can be 
#used to wait for the coprocess to terminate. Also, because it is an asynchronous command, the coproc command 
#always returns success. The return status of a coprocess is the exit status of command.    
